{
"questions": [
{"id": "q1", "target_chain_ids": ["base_c2", "base_c3"], "focus": "observations", "question": "Does the visible area of the red 'Xiaomi (20 %)' slice actually exceed the visible area of the orange 'Others (10 %)' slice, which appears to span a wider angular range on the right side of the pie chart?"},
{"id": "q2", "target_chain_ids": ["base_c1", "base_c2", "base_c3"], "focus": "rule_conditions", "question": "Is there a stated rule or public task condition that prioritizes the printed percentage labels over the drawn slice geometry when the two disagree, or is the chart's internal inconsistency the only governing condition?"},
{"id": "q3", "target_chain_ids": ["base_c1", "base_c2", "base_c3"], "focus": "coverage", "question": "Do the existing chains adequately cover the possibility that the pie chart's slice geometry is simply drawn inaccurately and should be disregarded in favor of the printed labels, or is this interpretation left unaddressed?"}
],
"summary": "I inspected the pie chart and the initial argument set. The set covers the printed-label reading (Samsung 55%), the visible-geometry reading (Xiaomi largest), and the inconsistency condition. However, the visible-geometry claim that Xiaomi is the largest slice is questionable because the 'Others' slice appears to have a larger angular span. Additionally, the set lacks a rule for resolving the conflict between printed labels and drawn geometry, and does not explicitly cover the interpretation that the geometry is simply an inaccurate drawing to be ignored."
}
